@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+Coro 6.57 and PERL_RC_STACK
+
+The original test suite crashes during coroutine switches and reports
+attempts to free unreferenced scalars. Coro bypasses pp_entersub after the
+first call to a schedule-like function (SLF), so the normal XS wrapper
+cannot manage reference ownership for subsequent calls.
+
+The repair enables reference ownership on new coroutine stacks. Stack
+pushes distinguish the RC stack used by custom opcodes and SLF callbacks
+from the non-RC frame provided to ordinary XS functions. Removing SLF
+arguments transfers their references to the temporary stack, preserving
+the values needed by the prepare callback. Return values are reduced to
+the calling context with rpp_context, which releases discarded values.
+
+The first SLF call still enters through the XS wrapper. That wrapper
+copies arguments above the original frame, then removes the copy before
+pp_restore runs. The saved argument offset must refer to the original
+frame. Otherwise pp_restore leaves stale pointers on the active stack.
+
+Coro's private pad-list save records contain pointers and integer values,
+not SV references. They are pushed and popped directly and removed before
+Perl resumes. They must not pass through the reference-owning push helpers.
+
+Reusable migration guidance
+
+Custom opcodes need a stack ownership audit even when ordinary XS entry
+points work with Perl's compatibility wrapper. Audit stack creation,
+argument offsets retained across XS returns, deferred argument lifetimes,
+callback pushes, and scalar/list/void result handling together. A wrapper
+around an opcode that switches coroutine stacks can retain offsets from
+the wrong coroutine; make ownership explicit in those paths.
+
+The changed ownership rules are conditional on PERL_RC_STACK. Other builds
+use the existing push macros. The helpers pass
+interpreter context through the existing pTHX/aTHX conventions and add no
+shared state. They use SV pointers without assumptions about NV size or
+representation. This repair does not make Coro safe to use from multiple
+interpreter threads; the restriction documented in Coro::State applies.
+
+Validation
+
+Fresh builds in separate library prefixes pass on x86_64 Linux:
+
+- Ordinary Perl v5.44.0, conventional stack.
+- Ordinary Perl v5.45.4, PERL_RC_STACK and DEBUGGING.
+- XPerl v5.45.5, commit 8903238ce74fc124ec407b8342791ef73253f988,
+  PERL_RC_STACK, no ithreads or DEBUGGING, NV double.
+
+All 21 test files (223 tests) pass on each build. The release suite and t/21_stack.t
+cover transfers, joins, channels,
+semaphores, signals, callbacks, cancellation, coroutine reuse and tracing.
+The focused stack regression also passes Valgrind with
+PERL_DESTRUCT_LEVEL=2 and --error-exitcode=97.
+
+Threaded, longdouble and quadmath builds have not been tested. Coro's
+experimental clone facility and optional EV,
+Event and IO::AIO integrations have not been validated by this repair.
+
+Upstream investigation
+
+The documented upstream uses CVS and the anyevent mailing list:
+https://software.schmorp.de/pkg/Coro.html
+https://cvs.schmorp.de/Coro/Coro/State.xs?revision=1.483&view=markup
+
+The current CVS code has no RC-stack migration. Perl's initial RC-stack
+report identifies Coro as failing:
+https://www.nntp.perl.org/group/perl.perl5.porters/2023/02/msg265801.html
+
+Perl issue 21784 concerns a different failure in EV/Event tied filehandle
+handling, fixed in core in 2024:
+https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/21784
+
+No upstream patch was adapted. The installer research report records the
+other sources checked and the endpoints that could not be fetched.
